{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790429258,"nonce":"lqBafbStXgYy/2UutgR8FqsA1UU=","message":"Notes from caribou's wasm prep (caribou git-bug f98f9f0: e964da6, 3bfe4a0, 7729de0) that bear on this epic:\n\n- Roots (stage 6): Ash pins every pointer-typed local of compiled code to a linear-memory shadow stack (TargetAbi::pointer_registers_in_memory, pin_register in ash/crates/ash/src/llvm/function/mod.rs). WrenLift's wasm output needs the same, or a shared heap frees live objects.\n- Runtime object (stage 1): under caribou, the wasm runtime object is caribou's one staticlib (ash_std + caribou + adapters + WrenLift's runtime half). Two copies of the runtime exports don't link. So WrenLift's runtime half must stay buildable as a library for wasm32-wasip1 with no JIT or compiler: the aot_runtime feature is that library. wlift_runtime.o stays the standalone case.\n- Linker (stage 4): Ash's ash_wasm_link takes several program objects plus one prelinked runtime object, so a WrenLift object can sit beside Ash's. That questions whether stage 4 should write its own two-object linker or use ash_wasm_link. Decide before starting stage 4.\n- Seams: under AOT/wasm caribou fills its seams from a static constructor, run in the start section (__wasm_call_ctors) before main. The bootstrap must not assume it runs first.\n- Dynamic calls (stage 3): one trampoline per signature, as the epic says (Ash aot_trampoline.rs; caribou 7729de0).\n- Cross-language symbols follow caribou::link's symbol and signature rule (caribou docs/architecture/linking.md).\n- Exceptions use setjmp lowered to wasm EH (Ash docs/internals/wasm-exceptions.md). Fibers go through env.ash_host_fiber_yield, or the linker transform under ASH_WASM_FIBERS.","files":null}]}